  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $6$ and $9$, respectively,  the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ is{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $6,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{6}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $9,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{9}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{6}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{9}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{6}\\cdot {x}^{9}={x}^{6+9}={x}^{15}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $15.$",
